1
00:00:00,330 --> 00:00:03,346
The runners at San Quentin cover twenty six point

2
00:00:03,366 --> 00:00:06,045
two miles without ever leaving the prison yard.

3
00:00:06,065 --> 00:00:08,406
That is one hundred and five laps.

4
00:00:08,426 --> 00:00:09,755
Past the same walls,

5
00:00:09,775 --> 00:00:12,117
the same corners, the same men watching,

6
00:00:12,137 --> 00:00:13,804
one hundred and five times.

7
00:00:14,524 --> 00:00:16,954
One of those runners would later line up at

8
00:00:16,974 --> 00:00:18,859
the Boston Marathon. As a free man.

9
00:00:18,879 --> 00:00:21,310
And run it faster than he ever ran inside.

10
00:00:22,030 --> 00:00:24,987
And last November, two prisoners did something no one

11
00:00:25,007 --> 00:00:26,971
had done in the race's history.

12
00:00:26,991 --> 00:00:29,286
They finished the marathon under three hours.

13
00:00:29,306 --> 00:00:30,279
One second apart.

14
00:00:31,599 --> 00:00:34,128
So how did a marathon end up inside one

15
00:00:34,148 --> 00:00:35,544
of America's most famous prisons?

16
00:00:35,564 --> 00:00:38,093
And what happens to the men who run it,

17
00:00:38,113 --> 00:00:39,510
when the gate finally opens?

18
00:00:41,250 --> 00:00:43,948
Chapter 1. California's oldest prison.

19
00:00:46,048 --> 00:00:49,179
San Quentin sits on a point of land in

20
00:00:49,199 --> 00:00:51,281
Marin County, on San Francisco Bay.

21
00:00:51,301 --> 00:00:53,382
It opened in eighteen fifty two.

22
00:00:53,402 --> 00:00:55,133
It is California's oldest prison.

23
00:00:55,853 --> 00:00:58,578
For decades, its name meant one thing.

24
00:00:58,598 --> 00:01:02,107
Death row. The state's condemned men were held here.

25
00:01:02,127 --> 00:01:04,068
California's execution chamber was here.

26
00:01:04,788 --> 00:01:06,332
That began to change.

27
00:01:06,352 --> 00:01:09,851
In twenty nineteen, Governor Gavin Newsom ordered a moratorium

28
00:01:09,871 --> 00:01:12,588
on executions, and the chamber was closed.

29
00:01:12,608 --> 00:01:14,152
In twenty twenty three,

30
00:01:14,172 --> 00:01:17,279
he announced that San Quentin would be remade,

31
00:01:17,299 --> 00:01:20,016
and renamed. The San Quentin Rehabilitation Center.

32
00:01:20,736 --> 00:01:21,997
By twenty twenty four,

33
00:01:22,017 --> 00:01:24,880
most of the men on death row had been

34
00:01:24,900 --> 00:01:26,161
moved to other prisons.

35
00:01:26,181 --> 00:01:27,762
Their sentences did not change.

36
00:01:27,782 --> 00:01:29,043
Only their address did.

37
00:01:29,763 --> 00:01:31,581
San Quentin was already unusual.

38
00:01:31,601 --> 00:01:33,051
In nineteen sixty nine,

39
00:01:33,071 --> 00:01:35,624
Johnny Cash recorded a live album here,

40
00:01:35,644 --> 00:01:37,462
in front of the prisoners.

41
00:01:37,482 --> 00:01:40,770
At San Quentin. It became one of the best

42
00:01:40,790 --> 00:01:43,343
known concerts ever held inside a prison.

43
00:01:44,063 --> 00:01:47,852
In later years, the prison became known for programs

44
00:01:47,872 --> 00:01:49,544
that barely exist elsewhere.

45
00:01:49,564 --> 00:01:51,660
A newspaper, San Quentin News,

46
00:01:51,680 --> 00:01:54,200
written and edited by incarcerated journalists.

47
00:01:54,220 --> 00:01:56,739
A college, run inside the walls,

48
00:01:56,759 --> 00:01:59,278
Mount Tamalpais College. And a podcast,

49
00:01:59,298 --> 00:02:03,087
Ear Hustle, created in twenty seventeen by a prisoner,

50
00:02:03,107 --> 00:02:05,203
Earlonne Woods, and an artist,

51
00:02:05,223 --> 00:02:07,319
Nigel Poor. In twenty twenty,

52
00:02:07,339 --> 00:02:10,705
it became a finalist for the Pulitzer Prize.

53
00:02:11,425 --> 00:02:14,726
Woods himself was serving thirty one years to life.

54
00:02:14,746 --> 00:02:18,047
In twenty eighteen, Governor Jerry Brown commuted his sentence.

55
00:02:18,067 --> 00:02:20,262
He kept working on the podcast,

56
00:02:20,282 --> 00:02:21,369
from the outside.

57
00:02:22,089 --> 00:02:23,998
But long before any of that,

58
00:02:24,018 --> 00:02:26,892
a different kind of program had already started on

59
00:02:26,912 --> 00:02:29,143
the yard. It began with one teacher,

60
00:02:29,163 --> 00:02:30,429
and one running coach.

61
00:02:31,969 --> 00:02:34,528
Chapter 2. The thousand mile club.

62
00:02:36,628 --> 00:02:38,078
In two thousand five,

63
00:02:38,098 --> 00:02:41,386
Laura Bowman, who worked with community programs at San

64
00:02:41,406 --> 00:02:44,693
Quentin, reached out to a local running club in

65
00:02:44,713 --> 00:02:46,163
Marin, the Tamalpa Runners.

66
00:02:46,883 --> 00:02:48,322
One of its members,

67
00:02:48,342 --> 00:02:50,146
Frank Ruona, volunteered to coach.

68
00:02:50,166 --> 00:02:53,064
He would stay for the next eighteen years.

69
00:02:53,784 --> 00:02:56,365
They called it the one thousand mile club.

70
00:02:56,385 --> 00:02:57,666
As Ruona later explained,

71
00:02:57,686 --> 00:02:58,967
the idea was simple.

72
00:02:58,987 --> 00:03:01,893
The men would try to run one thousand miles

73
00:03:01,913 --> 00:03:03,844
while they were at San Quentin.

74
00:03:04,564 --> 00:03:06,836
The club runs on a calendar.

75
00:03:06,856 --> 00:03:08,746
A benchmark mile in January.

76
00:03:08,766 --> 00:03:10,274
Three miles in February.

77
00:03:10,294 --> 00:03:12,183
Six in March. Then eight,

78
00:03:12,203 --> 00:03:14,857
then ten. A track meet in summer.

79
00:03:14,877 --> 00:03:16,767
A half marathon in July.

80
00:03:16,787 --> 00:03:18,294
A two hour run,

81
00:03:18,314 --> 00:03:20,204
then a three hour run.

82
00:03:20,924 --> 00:03:24,014
And in November, the race the whole year builds

83
00:03:24,034 --> 00:03:25,050
toward. The marathon.

84
00:03:26,370 --> 00:03:29,579
Outside volunteers lead the workouts and run the races.

85
00:03:29,599 --> 00:03:31,732
But the prisoners run the club.

86
00:03:31,752 --> 00:03:33,884
They elect officers, recruit new members,

87
00:03:33,904 --> 00:03:36,396
keep the roster, and record every mile.

88
00:03:37,116 --> 00:03:38,673
Today, according to the club,

89
00:03:38,693 --> 00:03:40,882
it has more than a hundred members.

90
00:03:40,902 --> 00:03:43,090
The youngest are in their early twenties.

91
00:03:43,110 --> 00:03:44,668
The oldest, in their seventies.

92
00:03:45,388 --> 00:03:46,726
When members are released,

93
00:03:46,746 --> 00:03:49,103
the club does not let them go.

94
00:03:49,123 --> 00:03:50,461
According to its website,

95
00:03:50,481 --> 00:03:53,517
released runners are given a membership in the Tamalpa

96
00:03:53,537 --> 00:03:56,573
Runners, the same club whose volunteers coached them inside.

97
00:03:57,293 --> 00:03:59,525
Ruona summed up his reason for coming back,

98
00:03:59,545 --> 00:04:01,213
week after week, in one line.

99
00:04:01,233 --> 00:04:02,620
I am my brother's keeper.

100
00:04:02,640 --> 00:04:03,746
If he needs help,

101
00:04:03,766 --> 00:04:05,716
I'm going to try to help him.

102
00:04:06,656 --> 00:04:09,784
Chapter 3. One hundred and five laps.

103
00:04:11,884 --> 00:04:14,638
So what does a marathon inside a prison actually

104
00:04:14,658 --> 00:04:15,254
look like?

105
00:04:15,974 --> 00:04:17,830
The race starts in the morning.

106
00:04:17,850 --> 00:04:19,705
In one year, at eight a.m.

107
00:04:19,725 --> 00:04:21,269
sharp, the runners lined up,

108
00:04:21,289 --> 00:04:24,082
and took off when the coaches' countdown reached one.

109
00:04:24,802 --> 00:04:27,559
The course is a loop around the prison yard.

110
00:04:27,579 --> 00:04:30,336
Twenty six point two miles works out to one

111
00:04:30,356 --> 00:04:31,570
hundred and five laps.

112
00:04:31,590 --> 00:04:34,038
About a quarter of a mile each time.

113
00:04:34,758 --> 00:04:36,718
It is not a smooth track.

114
00:04:36,738 --> 00:04:38,367
According to a PBS report,

115
00:04:38,387 --> 00:04:40,677
the loop has six ninety degree turns.

116
00:04:40,697 --> 00:04:42,656
Part of it runs on dirt,

117
00:04:42,676 --> 00:04:43,646
part on asphalt.

118
00:04:44,366 --> 00:04:45,927
The club's president, Tommy Wickerd,

119
00:04:45,947 --> 00:04:47,509
described one spot to PBS.

120
00:04:47,529 --> 00:04:48,774
Here in San Quentin,

121
00:04:48,794 --> 00:04:50,356
we call this the bottleneck.

122
00:04:50,376 --> 00:04:52,254
You go from dirt to asphalt.

123
00:04:52,274 --> 00:04:55,101
And if you step on a rock at twenty

124
00:04:55,121 --> 00:04:57,632
two miles, it's going to get your attention.

125
00:04:58,352 --> 00:05:00,438
And the runners are not alone.

126
00:05:00,458 --> 00:05:03,246
PBS described it as a crowded prison yard.

127
00:05:03,266 --> 00:05:05,703
Coaches and volunteers stand at the line,

128
00:05:05,723 --> 00:05:07,458
counting laps, handing out water,

129
00:05:07,478 --> 00:05:09,213
and calling out lap times.

130
00:05:09,933 --> 00:05:11,525
Counting is a serious job.

131
00:05:11,545 --> 00:05:14,428
Every runner has to be tracked for all one

132
00:05:14,448 --> 00:05:15,718
hundred and five laps.

133
00:05:15,738 --> 00:05:18,298
Miss one, and a man's marathon is wrong.

134
00:05:19,018 --> 00:05:22,116
Even getting the race to happen can be hard.

135
00:05:22,136 --> 00:05:25,234
In twenty eighteen, the marathon was postponed because of

136
00:05:25,254 --> 00:05:28,352
wildfire smoke. Then a lockdown canceled the new date.

137
00:05:28,372 --> 00:05:31,123
It was finally run in January twenty nineteen.

138
00:05:31,843 --> 00:05:33,979
That January race would set a record.

139
00:05:33,999 --> 00:05:36,751
And it would be the last San Quentin marathon

140
00:05:36,771 --> 00:05:38,291
for one runner in particular.

141
00:05:40,031 --> 00:05:41,882
Chapter 4. The gazelle.

142
00:05:43,982 --> 00:05:45,811
His name is Markelle Taylor.

143
00:05:45,831 --> 00:05:47,291
In two thousand one,

144
00:05:47,311 --> 00:05:49,510
he physically assaulted his pregnant girlfriend.

145
00:05:49,530 --> 00:05:51,360
Their son was born prematurely,

146
00:05:51,380 --> 00:05:54,689
and died. Taylor was convicted of second degree murder,

147
00:05:54,709 --> 00:05:57,279
and sentenced to fifteen years to life.

148
00:05:57,299 --> 00:05:58,758
He was twenty seven.

149
00:05:59,478 --> 00:06:02,336
A sentence of fifteen to life does not come

150
00:06:02,356 --> 00:06:03,615
with a release date.

151
00:06:03,635 --> 00:06:04,893
After the minimum term,

152
00:06:04,913 --> 00:06:07,451
a prisoner can go before the parole board.

153
00:06:07,471 --> 00:06:09,050
The board can say no.

154
00:06:09,070 --> 00:06:11,608
Again and again. Some men never get out.

155
00:06:12,328 --> 00:06:14,028
According to the New York Times,

156
00:06:14,048 --> 00:06:16,607
Taylor had been a victim of violence and sexual

157
00:06:16,627 --> 00:06:17,754
abuse as a child,

158
00:06:17,774 --> 00:06:19,187
and was dependent on alcohol.

159
00:06:19,207 --> 00:06:21,481
None of that changed what he had done.

160
00:06:21,501 --> 00:06:23,201
But it was where he started.

161
00:06:23,921 --> 00:06:25,810
In prison, he began to run.

162
00:06:25,830 --> 00:06:28,673
The Times described it as an antidote to despair,

163
00:06:28,693 --> 00:06:31,537
after a close friend in prison took his own

164
00:06:31,557 --> 00:06:31,855
life.

165
00:06:32,575 --> 00:06:34,197
He was fast. Very fast.

166
00:06:34,217 --> 00:06:37,154
The film director who later spent years with the

167
00:06:37,174 --> 00:06:38,469
club put it simply.

168
00:06:38,489 --> 00:06:39,783
He was the gazelle.

169
00:06:39,803 --> 00:06:40,768
The name stuck.

170
00:06:41,488 --> 00:06:42,736
According to his coach,

171
00:06:42,756 --> 00:06:45,587
he came to hold the club's records at every

172
00:06:45,607 --> 00:06:45,903
distance.

173
00:06:46,623 --> 00:06:49,280
And he had a goal that sounded almost impossible

174
00:06:49,300 --> 00:06:51,361
for a man serving a life sentence.

175
00:06:51,381 --> 00:06:52,550
In his own words.

176
00:06:52,570 --> 00:06:54,929
My goal is to run under three hours.

177
00:06:54,949 --> 00:06:56,416
Maybe qualify for Boston here.

178
00:06:56,436 --> 00:06:58,497
Be the first one to do that.

179
00:07:00,037 --> 00:07:01,656
Chapter 5. The record.

180
00:07:03,756 --> 00:07:05,552
On January eleventh, twenty nineteen,

181
00:07:05,572 --> 00:07:08,820
twenty nine runners lined up for the twelfth annual

182
00:07:08,840 --> 00:07:11,725
San Quentin marathon. A record for the club.

183
00:07:12,445 --> 00:07:15,286
One of them was a first timer named Fidelio

184
00:07:15,306 --> 00:07:16,875
Marin, forty eight years old.

185
00:07:16,895 --> 00:07:18,465
For the first five miles,

186
00:07:18,485 --> 00:07:20,054
he ran right beside Taylor.

187
00:07:20,774 --> 00:07:22,113
It did not last.

188
00:07:22,133 --> 00:07:24,152
Taylor pulled away, lap after lap.

189
00:07:24,172 --> 00:07:26,531
He crossed the line in three hours,

190
00:07:26,551 --> 00:07:28,571
ten minutes and forty two seconds.

191
00:07:28,591 --> 00:07:29,930
A new course record.

192
00:07:30,650 --> 00:07:33,446
Second place came more than twenty minutes later.

193
00:07:33,466 --> 00:07:35,558
Marin, in his very first marathon,

194
00:07:35,578 --> 00:07:36,262
finished third.

195
00:07:36,982 --> 00:07:39,091
Twenty three men finished that day.

196
00:07:39,111 --> 00:07:41,220
Another club record. The oldest finisher,

197
00:07:41,240 --> 00:07:42,995
according to the race results,

198
00:07:43,015 --> 00:07:46,188
was seventy one. The last man home finished in

199
00:07:46,208 --> 00:07:48,318
five hours and thirty three minutes.

200
00:07:48,338 --> 00:07:50,447
On that yard, every finish counted.

201
00:07:51,167 --> 00:07:52,519
One of the finishers,

202
00:07:52,539 --> 00:07:55,264
Steve Reitz, completed his first marathon that day.

203
00:07:55,284 --> 00:07:56,979
According to San Quentin News,

204
00:07:56,999 --> 00:07:58,695
it was his fourth attempt.

205
00:07:59,415 --> 00:08:02,225
Taylor's time was fast enough to qualify for Boston,

206
00:08:02,245 --> 00:08:05,055
at his age. But he was still a prisoner.

207
00:08:05,075 --> 00:08:07,571
And he had already been denied parole before.

208
00:08:08,291 --> 00:08:10,519
Then, about six weeks after the race,

209
00:08:10,539 --> 00:08:12,125
the parole board said yes.

210
00:08:13,865 --> 00:08:15,820
Chapter 6. Boston.

211
00:08:17,920 --> 00:08:19,412
In March twenty nineteen,

212
00:08:19,432 --> 00:08:22,057
Markelle Taylor walked out of San Quentin,

213
00:08:22,077 --> 00:08:23,569
after about eighteen years.

214
00:08:23,589 --> 00:08:26,214
The Boston Marathon was a month away.

215
00:08:26,934 --> 00:08:28,544
The qualifying deadline had passed.

216
00:08:28,564 --> 00:08:30,500
So he entered through a charity,

217
00:08:30,520 --> 00:08:32,130
and, according to Canadian Running,

218
00:08:32,150 --> 00:08:35,064
raised eight and a half thousand dollars to get

219
00:08:35,084 --> 00:08:35,716
a place.

220
00:08:36,436 --> 00:08:38,743
On April fifteenth, he started in the back,

221
00:08:38,763 --> 00:08:39,906
with the charity runners.

222
00:08:39,926 --> 00:08:42,524
A man who had trained for years in circles

223
00:08:42,544 --> 00:08:44,851
was now running a point to point course,

224
00:08:44,871 --> 00:08:46,887
from a small town to downtown Boston,

225
00:08:46,907 --> 00:08:48,341
with crowds the whole way.

226
00:08:49,061 --> 00:08:50,681
He finished in three hours,

227
00:08:50,701 --> 00:08:52,648
three minutes and fifty two seconds.

228
00:08:52,668 --> 00:08:55,599
Almost seven minutes faster than his record on the

229
00:08:55,619 --> 00:08:55,927
yard.

230
00:08:56,647 --> 00:08:58,970
For a man who had spent years running in

231
00:08:58,990 --> 00:09:01,313
circles, it was a very long way from the

232
00:09:01,333 --> 00:09:01,573
yard.

233
00:09:03,113 --> 00:09:04,871
Chapter 7. Outside.

234
00:09:06,971 --> 00:09:08,576
But freedom was not a finish line.

235
00:09:09,896 --> 00:09:12,927
After his release, Taylor lived in a reentry facility

236
00:09:12,947 --> 00:09:14,622
in San Francisco's Tenderloin district.

237
00:09:14,642 --> 00:09:15,978
He got a job,

238
00:09:15,998 --> 00:09:18,691
and then lost it when a contract ended.

239
00:09:18,711 --> 00:09:21,742
Life outside was hard in ways a marathon is

240
00:09:21,762 --> 00:09:22,081
not.

241
00:09:22,801 --> 00:09:25,994
He kept running. In September twenty twenty one,

242
00:09:26,014 --> 00:09:28,002
he finally broke three hours.

243
00:09:28,022 --> 00:09:30,010
Two hours, fifty six minutes,

244
00:09:30,030 --> 00:09:32,420
at a race among California's redwoods.

245
00:09:33,140 --> 00:09:34,794
In April twenty twenty two,

246
00:09:34,814 --> 00:09:37,472
a week after he was finally off parole,

247
00:09:37,492 --> 00:09:39,147
he went back to Boston.

248
00:09:39,167 --> 00:09:42,160
This time, he finished in two hours and fifty

249
00:09:42,180 --> 00:09:43,834
two minutes. According to CBS,

250
00:09:43,854 --> 00:09:46,847
that put him in the top five percent of

251
00:09:46,867 --> 00:09:47,182
runners.

252
00:09:47,902 --> 00:09:49,652
By then, according to the Times,

253
00:09:49,672 --> 00:09:51,127
he had his own apartment,

254
00:09:51,147 --> 00:09:52,897
a job at a grocery store,

255
00:09:52,917 --> 00:09:54,667
and twenty one years of sobriety.

256
00:09:54,687 --> 00:09:57,322
He had been elected to the board of the

257
00:09:57,342 --> 00:09:59,976
same running club whose volunteers had coached him in

258
00:09:59,996 --> 00:10:00,271
prison.

259
00:10:00,991 --> 00:10:03,382
And he went back to San Quentin.

260
00:10:03,402 --> 00:10:05,448
This time as a volunteer coach.

261
00:10:05,468 --> 00:10:07,859
He calls himself an ambassador for lifers.

262
00:10:08,579 --> 00:10:09,817
In twenty twenty three,

263
00:10:09,837 --> 00:10:12,649
PBS found him working full time at a supermarket,

264
00:10:12,669 --> 00:10:14,851
training for the New York City Marathon,

265
00:10:14,871 --> 00:10:17,683
and showing the film about his club to young

266
00:10:17,703 --> 00:10:17,998
offenders.

267
00:10:18,718 --> 00:10:20,740
He was not the only one.

268
00:10:20,760 --> 00:10:23,124
Other former members kept racing after release.

269
00:10:23,144 --> 00:10:24,485
In twenty twenty two,

270
00:10:24,505 --> 00:10:26,528
according to the Prison Journalism Project,

271
00:10:26,548 --> 00:10:29,252
nine club runners took part in the Dipsea,

272
00:10:29,272 --> 00:10:31,975
one of the oldest trail races in America,

273
00:10:31,995 --> 00:10:33,677
over the hills of Marin.

274
00:10:34,397 --> 00:10:36,895
CBS asked him what he would want written on

275
00:10:36,915 --> 00:10:38,014
his tombstone. He fell,

276
00:10:38,034 --> 00:10:39,693
he said, but he got up.

277
00:10:39,713 --> 00:10:42,211
And he changed the lives of a lot of

278
00:10:42,231 --> 00:10:43,610
human beings for the better.

279
00:10:45,350 --> 00:10:47,131
Chapter 8. Lockdown.

280
00:10:49,231 --> 00:10:51,254
While Taylor was running on the outside,

281
00:10:51,274 --> 00:10:53,298
the yard he had left went quiet.

282
00:10:54,018 --> 00:10:56,723
The yard had seen one more race first.

283
00:10:56,743 --> 00:10:58,085
In December twenty nineteen,

284
00:10:58,105 --> 00:11:01,151
the next San Quentin marathon was won by Mark

285
00:11:01,171 --> 00:11:03,195
Jarosik, the runner up from January.

286
00:11:03,215 --> 00:11:04,898
According to the Associated Press,

287
00:11:04,918 --> 00:11:07,964
he finished about six minutes slower than Taylor's record.

288
00:11:07,984 --> 00:11:11,029
The runners that day ranged from twenty two to

289
00:11:11,049 --> 00:11:12,392
seventy two years old.

290
00:11:13,112 --> 00:11:16,348
In twenty twenty, COVID swept through San Quentin,

291
00:11:16,368 --> 00:11:19,604
after a transfer of prisoners from another prison.

292
00:11:19,624 --> 00:11:21,638
It became a major outbreak.

293
00:11:21,658 --> 00:11:23,266
Prisoners died. Programs stopped.

294
00:11:23,986 --> 00:11:25,933
According to the New York Times,

295
00:11:25,953 --> 00:11:28,883
the pandemic curtailed the club's activities for more than

296
00:11:28,903 --> 00:11:29,539
two years.

297
00:11:30,259 --> 00:11:32,288
For a club built on counting miles,

298
00:11:32,308 --> 00:11:34,337
it was a long time without any.

299
00:11:35,657 --> 00:11:37,427
When the runners came back,

300
00:11:37,447 --> 00:11:39,574
the prison around them was changing.

301
00:11:39,594 --> 00:11:41,006
The rehabilitation center plan.

302
00:11:41,026 --> 00:11:42,438
Death row emptying out.

303
00:11:42,458 --> 00:11:45,301
And their coach getting ready to step back.

304
00:11:46,241 --> 00:11:48,277
Chapter 9. Under three hours.

305
00:11:50,377 --> 00:11:53,094
At the end of twenty twenty three,

306
00:11:53,114 --> 00:11:55,439
Frank Ruona retired as head coach,

307
00:11:55,459 --> 00:11:58,176
after eighteen years. Other volunteers took over,

308
00:11:58,196 --> 00:12:00,912
including longtime coaches from the Tamalpa club.

309
00:12:01,632 --> 00:12:04,357
By then, the club had a wider audience.

310
00:12:04,377 --> 00:12:07,445
A documentary called twenty six point two to Life,

311
00:12:07,465 --> 00:12:08,817
by director Christine Yoo,

312
00:12:08,837 --> 00:12:11,905
who spent six years visiting and filming the club,

313
00:12:11,925 --> 00:12:14,650
followed the runners through a San Quentin marathon.

314
00:12:14,670 --> 00:12:17,738
It was shown on the prison's own TV network,

315
00:12:17,758 --> 00:12:19,796
and later streamed on ESPN plus.

316
00:12:20,516 --> 00:12:23,275
In it, viewers met men like Rahsaan Thomas,

317
00:12:23,295 --> 00:12:25,359
serving fifty five years to life.

318
00:12:25,379 --> 00:12:28,139
He was the slowest runner in the film,

319
00:12:28,159 --> 00:12:31,265
and finished last. Governor Newsom later commuted his sentence,

320
00:12:31,285 --> 00:12:32,654
after twenty three years.

321
00:12:33,374 --> 00:12:36,582
Thomas was also a co host of Ear Hustle,

322
00:12:36,602 --> 00:12:38,734
the podcast made inside San Quentin.

323
00:12:38,754 --> 00:12:40,886
Running and storytelling, it turned out,

324
00:12:40,906 --> 00:12:42,679
came from the same yard.

325
00:12:43,399 --> 00:12:45,695
And Tommy Wickerd, the club president,

326
00:12:45,715 --> 00:12:49,168
serving a fifty seven year sentence for murder and

327
00:12:49,188 --> 00:12:51,484
gang related crimes. According to PBS,

328
00:12:51,504 --> 00:12:54,186
his release date is twenty forty five,

329
00:12:54,206 --> 00:12:56,116
unless he is paroled sooner.

330
00:12:56,836 --> 00:12:58,521
Then came November twentieth,

331
00:12:58,541 --> 00:13:02,356
twenty twenty five. The sixteenth annual San Quentin marathon.

332
00:13:02,376 --> 00:13:04,060
Seventy seven men started.

333
00:13:04,080 --> 00:13:06,191
A record. Thirty eight finished.

334
00:13:06,211 --> 00:13:07,469
Also a record.

335
00:13:08,189 --> 00:13:10,789
At the front, two runners stayed together,

336
00:13:10,809 --> 00:13:14,156
lap after lap, all the way to the end.

337
00:13:14,176 --> 00:13:17,150
Hugo Dimas crossed the line in two hours,

338
00:13:17,170 --> 00:13:19,769
fifty three minutes and forty four seconds.

339
00:13:19,789 --> 00:13:21,640
Jose Fajardo, one second later.

340
00:13:22,360 --> 00:13:23,647
Both broke three hours.

341
00:13:23,667 --> 00:13:25,281
According to San Quentin News,

342
00:13:25,301 --> 00:13:26,588
the prison's own newspaper,

343
00:13:26,608 --> 00:13:29,529
they were the first runners to do it in

344
00:13:29,549 --> 00:13:31,816
the race's history. Faster than the gazelle.

345
00:13:33,136 --> 00:13:35,289
Dimas described his race in one sentence.

346
00:13:35,309 --> 00:13:37,461
I stayed close, never lost my pace,

347
00:13:37,481 --> 00:13:39,944
and made my move at the right time.

348
00:13:40,664 --> 00:13:43,303
And the last finisher that day may have had

349
00:13:43,323 --> 00:13:44,780
the hardest race of all.

350
00:13:44,800 --> 00:13:46,258
Albert Campos Junior is deaf.

351
00:13:46,278 --> 00:13:48,917
He told the paper he hit the wall on

352
00:13:48,937 --> 00:13:50,689
lap forty four. He kept going.

353
00:13:50,709 --> 00:13:52,757
He finished in just under seven hours.

354
00:13:52,777 --> 00:13:54,235
Thirty eighth of thirty eight.

355
00:13:54,955 --> 00:13:57,888
A sign language interpreter helped him communicate with the

356
00:13:57,908 --> 00:13:59,529
coaches. According to the paper,

357
00:13:59,549 --> 00:14:01,498
he fought cramps through the forties,

358
00:14:01,518 --> 00:14:03,139
and prayed at lap seventy.

359
00:14:03,159 --> 00:14:05,108
Then he ran thirty five more.

360
00:14:06,048 --> 00:14:07,747
Chapter 10. Why run?

361
00:14:09,847 --> 00:14:11,673
There is a fair question here.

362
00:14:11,693 --> 00:14:13,826
These men were convicted of serious crimes.

363
00:14:13,846 --> 00:14:15,364
Some of them took lives.

364
00:14:15,384 --> 00:14:17,517
Why should they get to run marathons?

365
00:14:18,237 --> 00:14:20,845
The people behind the club do not claim that

366
00:14:20,865 --> 00:14:23,473
running erases anything. One of the coaches put it

367
00:14:23,493 --> 00:14:26,101
this way. I do believe people are able to

368
00:14:26,121 --> 00:14:28,437
change, and take responsibility for what they've done.

369
00:14:29,157 --> 00:14:31,510
What running offers is something prisons rarely do.

370
00:14:31,530 --> 00:14:32,993
A goal measured in miles.

371
00:14:33,013 --> 00:14:35,663
A number that only goes up if you show

372
00:14:35,683 --> 00:14:37,442
up. And a finish line that,

373
00:14:37,462 --> 00:14:38,629
unlike a parole date,

374
00:14:38,649 --> 00:14:40,112
is in your own hands.

375
00:14:40,832 --> 00:14:42,398
One hundred and five laps.

376
00:14:42,418 --> 00:14:43,984
Twenty six point two miles.

377
00:14:44,004 --> 00:14:46,205
Inside the walls of California's oldest prison.

378
00:14:46,225 --> 00:14:48,743
Some men run it to pass the time.

379
00:14:48,763 --> 00:14:50,646
Some run it to prove something.

380
00:14:50,666 --> 00:14:53,501
And at least one ran it all the way

381
00:14:53,521 --> 00:14:54,136
to Boston.

382
00:14:55,656 --> 00:14:56,842
What do you think?

383
00:14:56,862 --> 00:14:58,652
Should prisons run programs like this,

384
00:14:58,672 --> 00:15:00,764
or not? Tell us in the comments.

385
00:15:00,784 --> 00:15:03,178
And for a very different kind of prison,

386
00:15:03,198 --> 00:15:05,591
built to break men rather than train them,

387
00:15:05,611 --> 00:15:07,099
watch our story about CECOT,

388
00:15:07,119 --> 00:15:08,004
on this channel.
